  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 1200 PM MDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nA ridge-dominated pattern will result in an uncharacteristically\nwarm week through at least Saturday, featuring departures from\nnormal of up to 15-20 degrees across the lowlands both Monday and\nTuesday. Today, a southerly and easterly low-level post-frontal flow\npattern together with the more western ridge axis placement will \nsee us \"only\" climb into the upper 70's to lower 80's in the \nplains. A modest amount of mid-level moisture trapped beneath the \nridge may support an isolated shower this afternoon in and around\nPark County, but given the lack of forcing, any precipitation \nwould be light.\n\nThe ridge nudges eastward Monday, with the return of subsident\nflow and a light downslope wind component pushing temperatures to\nnear daily records for many. It's probable (>75% chance) that we \nwill register a few 90F+ readings across portions of the urban \ncorridor and South Platte River Valley. Once again, a spotty light\nafternoon shower can't be ruled out for parts of the high country\nwith lingering moisture around.\n\nCome Tuesday we'll see a slight relaxation of the ridge, with \nheight falls leading to modest cooling aloft and thus marginally \nunstable conditions in the high country. This should allow for a \nminor uptick in afternoon showers over the mountains, and perhaps \na weak thunderstorm or two. Elsewhere, moisture will be much too \nlimited to support any precipitation, although a little more cloud\ncover can be expected. With surface winds taking on a more \nupslope component, temperatures look to cool a couple of degrees \nover the plains/urban corridor, but will remain plenty warm.\n\nGeneral flattening of the ridge Wednesday through Saturday may \nallow for a few degrees of additional cooling, however ensembles \nremain locked-in on the continuation of well above normal \ntemperatures. It appears we'll hold on to sufficient mid-level \nmoisture to support slight chances (10-25%) of an afternoon shower\nin the high country essentially each day under a more westerly \nflow pattern.\n\nThere is broad agreement among both deterministic and ensemble\nguidance in the development of longwave troughing across the \nIntermountain West over the weekend, with a resultant pattern\nchange for Colorado come Sunday or Monday as said trough pushes\neast. Forecast uncertainty skyrockets starting Sunday, with\ncontinued variability in trough amplitude and speed among the\nrange of available guidance. Cluster analysis using the 10/4 0Z\nguidance favors the slower solutions by roughly 2:1. Whatever the\ncase, run-to-run trends suggest a rising potential for widespread\nprecipitation in the October 11-13 period, accompanied by a \nnotable cooldown. As is typical with these flow patterns, impacts\nwould be favored earlier for our mountains versus the lower\nelevations, so the current forecast does introduce higher PoPs for\nthe high country beginning Sunday.",
